WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.140
Hey David, why is Andrew Yang surging in the
polls?

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.320
This is a good question.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.790
First we have to address the facts is Andrew
Yang surging in the polls as of this recording,

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.650
Andrew Yang has recently pulled three and
four in a couple of polls.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.949
Like for example, there's an Emerson poll
where Andrew Yang has pulled for, there's

00:00:21.949 --> 00:00:24.839
a Quinnipiac poll where Andrew Yang has pulled
three.

00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:30.909
There is a slightly older USA Today poll where
Andrew Yang has pulled three and he's averaging

00:00:30.909 --> 00:00:34.290
about 2.5% so at one point he was only pulling
1%.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.700
In that sense, Andrew Yang clearly has some
momentum.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.830
I think the better argument for talking about
Yang's appeal is the accelerated donor base

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:48.609
that Andrew Yang has picked up while many
other one and two percenters have not even

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:52.750
gotten enough donations to qualify for the
third debate that's coming up next week.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.359
Um, and uh, yes I think increased social media
attention also can tell us about interest

00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:01.160
in Andrew Yang if not electoral support.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.899
Do I think Andrew Yang is rocketing up in
the polls?

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:04.899
No.

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:07.860
I mean he's still averaging 2.5% and he's
made the third debate stage.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.860
That's great.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.860
I've interviewed him.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:10.860
I like him.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.860
I'm glad to see him there.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.750
I want to hear his ideas but there is not
evidence at this point that there was going

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:19.370
to be a much more head room so to speak for
Andrew Yang to take advantage of.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:23.190
Although I'm sure people hearing me talk about
this a will disagree.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:28.520
The reason I believe Andrew Yang is getting
attention is he is talking to people in a

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.980
relatable way.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:36.110
He sounds far less like a politician, but
not in a way that makes him sound like he's

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.610
delusional with ideas that are completely
disconnected from the real world.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:45.480
His ubi plan, you can agree with it or disagree
with it, you can find areas of it that you

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.230
think are more or less feasible or more or
less advantageous.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:56.080
But it is a very specific policy that he has
outlined now thousands of times as he has

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.490
been running this election.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.710
And it is resonating with people even if they
don't necessarily think that's the best way

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:06.470
to spend government money that he is putting
out specific and actionable plans.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.610
So I think it's a combination of personality,
demeanor and the fact that he is at least

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:17.440
attempting to stick to policy and he has many
policies out there that uh, many people are

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.680
liking.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.000
Is every single one of them completely feasible?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.950
No, that's the case with any presidential
candidate.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:31.150
Is Andrew Yang going to get enough speaking
time at the debate such that he would even

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:37.630
have the opportunity to really be as prominently
placed as Joe Biden, Warren or Bernie?

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:38.630
He's not.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:39.630
And you can argue that's not fair.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.270
I mean you can make the case that if there
are 10 candidates in a debate, regardless

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.781
of polling, each should get 10% of the time,
and I wouldn't completely disagree with you,

00:02:47.781 --> 00:02:50.930
even though I understand why the networks
do it the way they do it.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:56.300
I, I don't disagree with the premise that
if you really want the debates to be an adjudication

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.390
of all of the ideas that are out there, you
really should be giving the candidates equal

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.390
time.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:02.390
Although I get why they don't.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:03.450
So I think that's what it is.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:07.220
I think what Andrew Yang has done, I know
that he's talking about this is not to now

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.740
go away in a month.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.950
This is to become president of the United
States.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:15.061
I don't see how the numbers point to him doing
that now in the same way that I didn't see

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.750
it with Tulsi and Tulsi has not even made
the third debate and there are still people

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.400
saying she's going to be president.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.350
I think at some point we have to be a little
more realistic, but I think that Andrew Yang's

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.040
presidency has been good for the Democratic
Party.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.220
I think it's been good for elections in general.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.500
And I'm interested in seeing how far he goes.

